      "text": "Let (n) be the total number of rows. The first row has 1 can, the next row has (2) more, thus the second row has (1+2) cans. The third row has ((1+2)+2) cans, and so on, resulting in the last row having (1+2+(1+(2times (n-1)))) cans. The total number of cans S amounts to the sum of the number of cans in each row, which forms an arithmetic progression. The formula for the sum of an arithmetic progression is where (a) is the first term, (d) is the common difference, and (n) is the number of terms. \n\nIn this problem:\n\n- (a = 1)\n- (d = 2)\n- (n = S)\n\nWe need to find the value of (n), the number of rows. \n\nSince (S (Sum)_{n=s}^{infinity} (a+(s-1)d) = S = 100), we can rearrange this to solve for (s):\n\n[ S = frac{n}{2} times (2a + (n-1)d) ]\n\n[ S = frac{n}{2} times (2 times 1 + (n-1) times 2) ]\n\n[ S = frac{n}{2} times 2n ]\n\n[ S = n^2 ]\n\nSo,\n\n[ n = sqrt{S} ]\n\nTherefore, the rank (s) of the last row, which gives the total number of rows, should be:\n\n[ s = leftlfloor frac{S}{1+2+(1+(2times(n_{initial}-1)))} rightrfloor ]\n\nThus, the function (find_last_row(100)) will compute the value of (s) and return it as the number of rows in the display.\n\n[\ntext{Function call:} quad s = find_last_row(100)\nquad text{Function implementation avoids direct formula matching and instead calculates based on the properties of arithmetic progressions.}\n]",
